# Pages this archive draws by hand on purpose. checkpages prints every line
# of this file on every run, so an exemption nobody can justify is an
# exemption somebody will notice.

dna: four data files, and the subject is TWO COMPANIES DISAGREEING ABOUT THE SAME SALIVA — 23andMe read against MyHeritage, a match corpus, surname probes, and autoclusters subtracted to find a paternal group. No other archive in the estate has a second reading to compare against.
open-questions: written as PROSE, and the kit's Questions component says itself that prose "cannot use this until somebody extracts them, and that extraction is research work belonging to each archive rather than something to guess at from outside". These seven are multi-paragraph arguments with their own embedded links and lists — question 1 alone runs a hundred lines and turns on which of two readings of a clerk's phrase is right. Splitting them into what-is-known, what-has-been-tried and what-would-settle-it means deciding which sentence is which, about this archive's own material, and that is a judgement for whoever did the work.
places/[slug]: a TABLE OF EVERYONE OF THE SURNAME recorded in the place, with each one's birth year, nationality, occupation and the record that produced them. The kit's PlacePage lists what an archive holds about a place; this lists the people, and the columns are the point — a nationality column is how this archive tells two Llerena households apart.
